# Copy to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored; this file is the only one that is committed.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# NEVER put a real value in this file. Every entry here is a placeholder or a local-development
# credential that is already public in `docker-compose.yml`. Real secrets live in GitHub
# Environments and Fly secrets — see the README's "What a human has to set".
#
# `tests/env-example.test.ts` fails the build if this file drifts from the API's schema
# (`apps/api/src/config.ts`) or from the variables `docker-compose.yml` reads.

# ─── The API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every variable the API accepts is declared in `EnvSchema` in `apps/api/src/config.ts`, or it
# does not exist. The process refuses to start on a bad one — exit 78, naming every problem at
# once rather than the first.

# REQUIRED. The only variable with no default: the API will not boot without it.
# This value matches the local stack, so it works as-is after `pnpm stack:up`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://marketplace:marketplace_local@127.0.0.1:5432/marketplace

# Optional — each shown with the default the API uses when unset.
NODE_ENV=development
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3000
LOG_LEVEL=info
# Set by the deploy pipeline to the released tag or commit; surfaced by GET /health.
APP_VERSION=0.0.0-dev

# ─── The local stack ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host ports for `docker compose`. Override any of them if something else on your machine already
# owns the port — a Postgres from another project on 5432 is the usual one. Only the *host* side
# changes; the services still reach each other by container name, so nothing else needs editing.
#
# The credentials are `marketplace` / `marketplace_local` and are hard-coded in
# `docker-compose.yml` on purpose: they are local-only and must never be parameterised, or someone
# will eventually point them at something real.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
MAIL_SMTP_PORT=1025
MAIL_HTTP_PORT=8025
OBJECTS_PORT=9000
OBJECTS_CONSOLE_PORT=9001

# ─── Tests ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Opt in to the suites that need a running stack (`pnpm stack:up` first). Left unset, those
# criteria are skipped and `pnpm verify` needs no Docker daemon. CI sets it in the `database` job.
# STACK_LIVE=1

# ─── The web app ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vite only exposes variables prefixed `VITE_` to client code, and inlines them at BUILD time — a
# value set here is baked into the bundle, so it must never hold anything secret.
#
# Nothing reads this yet: `apps/web` has no API client until `W1-T03`. The deploy workflows
# already set it at build time, so the first slice that calls the API should read it rather than
# inventing its own name.
VITE_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000

# ─── Neon: an optional personal cloud database ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADR-006 gives you two local paths, deliberately. The default is the docker-compose Postgres
# above: fast, free, offline, and exactly what CI runs. The second is a personal Neon branch, for
# when the *shape* of real data matters:
#
#   neonctl branches create --name dev/<you>
#
# Then point DATABASE_URL at the branch it prints — the connection string has the same shape, so
# nothing in the app knows the difference. `neonctl` reads these two:
#
#   NEON_API_KEY=
#   NEON_PROJECT_ID=
#
# Both are commented out because you do not need them to work on this repo, and because a value
# here is a value that can be committed by accident. Use `neonctl auth` instead where you can.
# Branch from a **sanitised** parent, never from production (ADR-006 / W0-T20).

# ─── Deploy credentials — names only, set in GitHub, never here ───────────────────────────────
# Listed so the inventory is in one place and a new one cannot be introduced without appearing
# here — `tests/env-example.test.ts` cross-checks this list against every `${{ secrets.* }}` the
# workflows read AND against `REQUIRED` in `scripts/deploy/config.ts`, and fails if any of the
# three disagree.
#
# Every one of them is commented out and empty, and must stay that way. They are GitHub
# Environment secrets: nothing you run on this machine uses one, and no agent may ever create,
# read, echo or commit a value.
#
#   Where: Settings → Environments → <environment> → Add secret
#   Status: none of these exists yet — issue #156 (W0-T24) is the activation checklist.
#
# preview — a Fly app, a Neon branch and a Pages preview per pull request
#   FLY_API_TOKEN=
#   CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
#   NEON_API_KEY=
#   NEON_PROJECT_ID=
#   PREVIEW_DATABASE_URL=
#
# staging — main, deployed on every merge
#   STAGING_DATABASE_URL=
#
# production — the shortest list on purpose: no Cloudflare or Neon key, so a release cannot
# create or destroy a database branch. Blast radius is a function of what the token can reach.
#   PRODUCTION_DATABASE_URL=
